WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:03.127
[gun banging]

00:00:04.264 --> 00:00:05.795
Steven Mason: He probably
should've had some camouflage

00:00:05.795 --> 00:00:08.480
on there and not just a
giant white bald head.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.473
My name is Sergeant
First Class Steven Mason.

00:00:10.473 --> 00:00:11.720
I'm the senior jungle instructor

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:13.758
for Jungle Operation Training course

00:00:13.758 --> 00:00:16.300
at the Lightning Academy in
the 25th Infantry Division.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.040
I've currently been the senior instructor

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:20.200
for two-plus years out there,

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.740
and I specialize in tracking and survival.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:26.770
Today we're gonna be going
over some jungle-warfare movies

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:28.595
to determine whether or not they're real.

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:37.503
[soldier moaning]

00:00:39.553 --> 00:00:41.349
This movie is ridiculous.

00:00:41.349 --> 00:00:43.250
That guy has the inability to talk

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:44.880
just because he's being
pulled into something.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.837
He's like, "Oh, oh, oh, oh!" [laughs]

00:00:46.837 --> 00:00:49.062
[dramatic music]

00:00:50.871 --> 00:00:52.870
I would say that the only time

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:56.420
that that would ever happen is, uh, never.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:58.690
A camouflage top would be better.

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:01.225
He would have been leaving
dinosaur-size tracks.

00:01:01.225 --> 00:01:02.670
So, a lot of people, when
they think about tracking,

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:04.530
they just think about, like,
a footprint on the ground,

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.592
but that's actually one of
the last things we look for.

00:01:07.592 --> 00:01:09.307
Everything is so thick
that you're actually

00:01:09.307 --> 00:01:11.706
leaving most of your imprints from, like,

00:01:11.706 --> 00:01:12.714
your knee to your shoulder.

00:01:12.714 --> 00:01:14.410
And that's where you're
kind of maneuvering around

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:16.126
and bending leaves and turning leaves

00:01:16.126 --> 00:01:17.951
and leaving scratches.

00:01:17.951 --> 00:01:19.483
So, I mean, he would have done

00:01:19.483 --> 00:01:21.241
a significant amount of that kind of stuff

00:01:21.241 --> 00:01:22.741
walking in and out of there.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:25.994
[man yelling]

00:01:25.994 --> 00:01:28.620
We teach a lot about water sources

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.750
and the way to move about them

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.210
and use them to your advantage.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:34.360
I don't know that we teach
coming out of the water

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.330
and shooting people with a bow
and arrow that makes noise.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:39.312
So, we like to use
waterways to traverse them.

00:01:39.312 --> 00:01:41.545
So instead of having to walk through,

00:01:41.545 --> 00:01:43.910
for example, that jungle
in the background,

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:45.460
that would be very taxing on the body

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:46.630
if you had to walk

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:48.550
a significant amount of
distance through that,

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:51.100
where we just teach you
to get in that waterway

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:53.430
and move until you think
you can get out again

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.550
and start walking, like they're doing.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.540
But I would never say
that there's a scenario

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.380
where you'd have to,
like, hide in the water.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:01.130
So if I was Rambo in this situation,

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.770
I would probably be in
the rocks where he was,

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:05.250
and that way if they do start shooting,

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:06.380
you at least have cover.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:07.421
But once they come to you,

00:02:07.421 --> 00:02:09.387
then that's it. That's game over.

00:02:09.387 --> 00:02:11.275
I've been put in situations in the jungle

00:02:11.275 --> 00:02:13.573
where they give you

00:02:13.573 --> 00:02:16.480
a couple days or a week worth
of classroom environment,

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.980
and then they load you
up in the back of a truck

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.010
and they drop you off in
the jungle by yourself,

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:23.670
and you have nothing besides
a knife and a machete

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.726
for the next 10 to 14 days.

00:02:25.726 --> 00:02:27.500
Survival isn't what people think it is

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:29.117
whenever they watch, like, Bear Grylls.

00:02:29.117 --> 00:02:30.620
You know, like, he's excellent at it,

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:32.468
but he had to have gotten to that point.

00:02:32.468 --> 00:02:33.873
But you just can't go into
the woods and be like,

00:02:33.873 --> 00:02:36.043
"I'm gonna survive!" and then crush it.

00:02:36.043 --> 00:02:38.817
I'm gonna say a two out of 10,

00:02:38.817 --> 00:02:40.242
10 out of 10 for awesome.

00:02:42.291 --> 00:02:43.622
Spook: Is this a double feature?

00:02:43.622 --> 00:02:45.510
[soldiers laughing]

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:47.130
For God's sake, just lean your head back

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:48.610
and open your mouth!

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:50.470
Steven: That's pretty outdated.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:52.290
I mean, we all chuckle
through all of them,

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:55.834
but when stuff actually hits the fan,

00:02:55.834 --> 00:02:57.993
your brain reverts back to, like,

00:02:57.993 --> 00:03:00.119
"Oh, my gosh, I saw this stupid video,

00:03:00.119 --> 00:03:01.530
and that might save my life."

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:03.760
So, we do teach how to collect rainwater

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:06.240
in different types of leaves or bamboos.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.967
If you were to just tilt your
head back and open your mouth,

00:03:08.967 --> 00:03:11.270
you wouldn't get enough
water to do anything.

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:13.070
So, everyone talks
about the rule of three.

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:15.370
So you can go three minutes without air,

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:16.490
three days without water,

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:17.860
three weeks without food.

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:18.880
But that's a good thing about the jungle,

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:19.910
there's water everywhere.

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:22.070
There's a lot of vines inside of jungles

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:24.740
that just are filled
with water for that tree.

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:26.480
And if you can find those,
just cut that in half

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.640
and you have a couple quarts of water.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:30.350
The rule of thumb for soldiers is

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:31.920
a quart of water an hour

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:33.867
if you're under a heavy workload.

00:03:33.867 --> 00:03:35.660
Narrator: Your best friend is your knife.

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:37.290
Spook: Look at that, it's getting serious.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.230
Steven: A knife is absolutely
the most important tool.

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:40.570
And knives are used for everything,

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:43.020
from building your shelter with it,

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:45.040
just using it to sharpen things,

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:46.680
to slash things, to cut things.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:47.520
It has to be durable.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.640
That's why we talk about full-tang blades,

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:53.740
meaning that the knife is
the full length of the handle

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:55.228
instead of just being the blade

00:03:55.228 --> 00:03:57.400
and then a little bit
goes into the handle.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:59.370
'Cause you need to be able
to put that into trees

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:02.276
and then bang a rock on it to split wood.

00:04:02.276 --> 00:04:04.665
It just needs to be used for everything.

00:04:04.665 --> 00:04:07.155
[helicopter whirring]

00:04:07.155 --> 00:04:10.540
[Dieter yelling and laughing]

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:11.680
Even though it's right above you,

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:13.230
you probably would forget about security

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:14.258
and running back into the woods

00:04:14.258 --> 00:04:16.480
until it actually was
time to get picked up.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:19.610
And at this point he's
just trying to live.

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:22.000
He did exactly what we
teach soldiers to do.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.340
If you need to get found
in a jungle environment,

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:26.760
you'd find a big open area
and try to try to create

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:28.500
something that's larger than yourself

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:29.860
so that you could be signaled.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:30.700
You could light a fire

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:32.050
and then put a lot of green leaves on it.

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:34.254
It'll create a big plume of white smoke.

00:04:34.254 --> 00:04:39.129
[helicopter whirring]
[Dieter yelling]

00:04:39.129 --> 00:04:40.300
That's a jungle penetrator.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:42.377
We actually use that
here at jungle school.

00:04:42.377 --> 00:04:44.300
Same technology from Vietnam.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.387
We're still using it today
'cause it's effective.

00:04:46.387 --> 00:04:47.837
Soldier: What's your authenticator?

00:04:47.837 --> 00:04:48.960
What's your authenticator?

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:50.303
Dieter: Rescue Dawn!
Soldier: Louder!

00:04:50.303 --> 00:04:51.978
Dieter: Rescue Dawn!

00:04:53.053 --> 00:04:54.153
Steven: That's very realistic.

00:04:54.153 --> 00:04:55.320
So, whenever you deploy,

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.310
you have to fill out some forms,

00:04:57.310 --> 00:04:59.940
and there's a sequence of
questions that you have to answer

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:01.610
in case this scenario does happen.

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:02.443
You even have to put, like,

00:05:02.443 --> 00:05:05.000
where your tattoos are on your body,

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.180
so when you do get caught, they, you know,

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:08.720
they're making sure they're
picking up the right person

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:10.479
and not someone they don't
want to be picking up.

00:05:10.479 --> 00:05:11.995
That's the thing about the jungle

00:05:11.995 --> 00:05:14.460
is that it can start
breaking you down physically

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:15.800
within 24 hours.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:17.570
And you can look like this within a week,

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:19.470
and it all depends on your skill set

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:20.940
and how long you can survive.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:23.555
Like, me, I think I could
survive pretty much indefinitely.

00:05:23.555 --> 00:05:25.441
I would say that the last scene,

00:05:25.441 --> 00:05:27.455
that's probably a 10 out of 10.

00:05:27.455 --> 00:05:29.612
Chris: A fake human being.

00:05:29.612 --> 00:05:31.483
Maybe I can see something I don't yet see

00:05:31.483 --> 00:05:34.020
or learn something I don't yet know.

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:36.528
Steven: This is my absolute
favorite jungle scene

00:05:36.528 --> 00:05:38.610
in any movie that's ever been produced,

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:39.970
because this sums up

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:43.269
what it's like to be
in the jungle, 1,000%.

00:05:43.269 --> 00:05:45.010
You are just wet and miserable,

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:46.670
and you're wondering the entire time

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:48.320
why you made this life choice.

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:50.050
Oliver Stone, the guy
that made this movie,

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:51.730
he was actually in the 25th Infantry

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:53.430
and deployed with them to Vietnam.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:58.600
It's usually pretty dark inside of there.

00:05:58.600 --> 00:05:59.620
I've been in a lot of jungles

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:02.030
where you can't even see your
hand in front of your face

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:03.330
during night operations.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:04.421
Kind of looks like, you know,

00:06:04.421 --> 00:06:06.849
they have a spotlight on top of him.

00:06:06.849 --> 00:06:09.124
[mosquito buzzing]

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:15.520
You just have to be good
at being uncomfortable.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:17.360
There's no trick to that trade.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:19.769
So, actually, the 25th,

00:06:19.769 --> 00:06:22.986
we were the first ones to
field the new jungle uniforms,

00:06:22.986 --> 00:06:25.751
a little bit lighter
weight that dry out faster.

00:06:25.751 --> 00:06:29.264
We have jungle boots that dry out quicker.

00:06:31.197 --> 00:06:32.189
[patting]

00:06:32.189 --> 00:06:34.513
Soldier: Junior, hey!

00:06:34.513 --> 00:06:36.179
Steven: I'm gonna be up scanning

00:06:36.179 --> 00:06:38.103
for every little movement or sound I can.

00:06:38.103 --> 00:06:39.220
So that keeps me up,

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:41.692
and I think that just the fear of

00:06:41.692 --> 00:06:43.370
everything that's crawling
around on the ground

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:45.190
while you're down there
on it also keeps you up.

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:47.100
50% of everything is trying to kill you,

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:48.680
whether it be the plant life or animals.

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:50.496
In Vietnam, they have, like,

00:06:50.496 --> 00:06:52.437
15 different poisonous snakes,

00:06:52.437 --> 00:06:54.120
double that in poisonous spiders.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:55.480
And then they have tarantulas.

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:58.330
So, yeah, you're just
constantly on the lookout

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:01.193
for what you don't want to
step on, sleep on, or grab.

00:07:06.664 --> 00:07:08.655
And another thing is
there's only one person

00:07:08.655 --> 00:07:10.280
up on guard on this patrol,

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:12.516
and that would never happen.

00:07:12.516 --> 00:07:15.470
[guns blasting]

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:18.155
So, in Vietnam, the
average gunfight, firefight

00:07:18.155 --> 00:07:19.530
was within 5 to 10 meters.

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:22.420
So the enemy was able
to get very close to us,

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:24.680
but we were also able to
get very close to the enemy.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:26.650
Stealth in the jungle is paramount

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:28.140
to probably anything else.

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:30.950
You actually use the same things today

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:31.783
as you did back then.

00:07:31.783 --> 00:07:33.603
So, technology has advanced a lot.

00:07:33.603 --> 00:07:36.527
So the jungle really doesn't
give you that capability,

00:07:36.527 --> 00:07:39.390
because satellites can't
penetrate through it.

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:40.700
The night vision can't get through there.

00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:42.040
'Cause I was talking about how it's dark,

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:44.320
so, night vision actually
needs ambient light to work.

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:46.862
So you need a little bit of
light to make night vision work.

00:07:46.862 --> 00:07:50.103
[wounded soldier screaming]

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:54.370
So, when you get shot,

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:56.550
if you're still in that
type of environment

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:59.870
and you haven't won the battle yet,

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:01.990
you have to figure out kinda
how to deal with the pain,

00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:03.970
like he said, so that you're
not giving away your position.

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:06.920
'Cause you don't know if the
enemy has left the area yet.

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:08.260
Actually a lot of people,
when they get hit,

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:09.290
they don't even realize they got hit,

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:10.570
'cause their adrenaline's just pumping

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:12.140
and they're just so ready for it.

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:13.730
And then someone actually
has to tell them, like,

00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:15.050
"Hey, man, you know you got shot?"

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:17.010
I've personally seen that, absolutely.

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:18.850
I would give this a 10 out of 10

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:20.964
just because this
reminds me of every squad

00:08:20.964 --> 00:08:22.235
I've ever been in.

00:08:23.252 --> 00:08:25.585
[gun blasting]

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:28.333
He probably should've had
some camouflage on there

00:08:28.333 --> 00:08:31.482
and not just a giant white bald head.

00:08:31.482 --> 00:08:32.630
Soldier: Keep your eye on the package.

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:34.310
Flea: Roger that. Holding.

00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:36.368
Steven: What was accurate about this

00:08:36.368 --> 00:08:38.801
is that they put a guy, a sniper,

00:08:38.801 --> 00:08:40.100
up top who was kind of like

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:41.810
their topside security overwatch,

00:08:41.810 --> 00:08:44.250
who was watching this scenario unfold,

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:46.346
who had coms with them, who could say,

00:08:46.346 --> 00:08:47.793
"Hey, the situation has changed.

00:08:47.793 --> 00:08:48.760
Don't go in."

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:49.910
The fact that you have to get

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:52.927
all the way up to the
village very stealthily

00:08:52.927 --> 00:08:56.370
and then try to work your way
in one building at a time,

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:57.640
that's pretty accurate.

00:08:57.640 --> 00:08:59.504
If you were gonna go
do something like that,

00:08:59.504 --> 00:09:02.157
an operation like that, you
would be camo-ed head to toe.

00:09:02.157 --> 00:09:03.760
But also them using suppressors,

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:05.518
that's pretty accurate.

00:09:05.518 --> 00:09:08.902
[suspenseful music]

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:12.427
You would use hand and arm signals a lot

00:09:12.427 --> 00:09:13.260
moving around the jungle.

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:15.770
A lot of them is just kind
of like the, you know,

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:18.486
halt, stop, keep moving,

00:09:18.486 --> 00:09:19.561
standard stuff.

00:09:19.561 --> 00:09:22.800
You know, these, these, stuff like that.

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:24.451
That's about it.

00:09:25.393 --> 00:09:27.490
Using rally points and pointing places

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:28.815
saying, you know, like, "Hey,

00:09:28.815 --> 00:09:31.024
this is something significant."

00:09:31.024 --> 00:09:33.452
You're gonna have communication on you.

00:09:33.452 --> 00:09:36.685
So, they have this cool-guy
stuff, with an earpiece.

00:09:36.685 --> 00:09:39.150
They even have stuff --
this, like, throat mic,

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:41.650
so you can just press on your
throat and you can whisper

00:09:41.650 --> 00:09:44.632
and it still comes across on
the other end just as loud.

00:09:44.632 --> 00:09:46.200
So, yeah, everything's pretty accurate,

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:47.640
other than the face paint.

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:49.960
I would give this a nine out of 10.

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:52.627
[guns blasting]

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:55.290
As soon as they made contact,

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:56.613
they immediately rolled into the ditch

00:09:56.613 --> 00:09:57.580
and then returned fire.

00:09:57.580 --> 00:09:58.813
They got behind the nearest cover

00:09:58.813 --> 00:10:00.180
and just started returning fire.

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:01.580
What was funny is that they were just

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:03.661
walking kind of in a straight line

00:10:03.661 --> 00:10:05.036
across this open danger area,

00:10:05.036 --> 00:10:05.880
which wouldn't have happened.

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:07.230
You could have done
another number of methods.

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:08.945
You could have bounded across it,

00:10:08.945 --> 00:10:10.760
or you could have, we
call it hand railing,

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:11.795
where you just kind of

00:10:11.795 --> 00:10:13.237
stay on the wood line and go through,

00:10:13.237 --> 00:10:16.090
and then that way you wouldn't
have been an open target.

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:18.050
It is funny that he gets on the radio

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:19.010
and just starts calling in

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:20.570
and then rounds just start dropping.

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:21.680
Doesn't even give a grid.

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:22.852
Doesn't tell them where he's at.

00:10:22.852 --> 00:10:23.980
Just says, "Tree line."

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:25.275
You're in the woods, and someone's like,

00:10:25.275 --> 00:10:27.589
"We're in the tree line,
let's go!" [laughs]

00:10:27.589 --> 00:10:28.989
Dan: Pull back!

00:10:29.955 --> 00:10:31.580
Pull back!

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:32.871
Steven: I'm not sure that

00:10:32.871 --> 00:10:35.021
the term "pull back" is an Army term.

00:10:35.021 --> 00:10:36.400
We would say "break contact."

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:38.330
We'd have somebody covering our fire

00:10:38.330 --> 00:10:39.960
while another group gets out of town.

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:41.830
And then once they get to
where they're gonna go,

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:42.890
they'll turn around and start shooting,

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:44.540
and then the next group leaves.

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:46.020
I don't think I've ever seen a situation

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:47.160
where someone yells, "Pull back!"

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:48.340
and then everyone just starts running

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:49.450
in the opposite direction.

00:10:49.450 --> 00:10:51.230
So, the responsibility of the leader

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:53.478
is to command and control
the entire situation.

00:10:53.478 --> 00:10:56.370
At no point, unless it's,
like, worst-case scenario,

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:59.740
should the leader of the entire patrol

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:01.292
be picking people up
and running with them.

00:11:01.292 --> 00:11:03.535
He should be delegating those tasks

00:11:03.535 --> 00:11:05.500
and then relaying all
the information to higher

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:06.510
and giving out guidance.

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:09.951
I would give this a six out of 10.

00:11:09.951 --> 00:11:12.029
Blain: Come on in.

00:11:12.029 --> 00:11:13.960
Ol' Painless is waiting.

00:11:16.580 --> 00:11:17.413
[sighs]

00:11:17.413 --> 00:11:18.613
Steven: After you've been in there awhile,

00:11:18.613 --> 00:11:21.640
you can start to tell
that the weight of a,

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:23.913
let's say a tiger isn't
the weight of a human.

00:11:23.913 --> 00:11:25.440
So you'll be able to distinguish the two.

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:27.670
Plus, a tiger knows how to
step, and a human doesn't.

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:29.370
Any snap or crack you hear,

00:11:29.370 --> 00:11:30.960
you're gonna automatically
be on high alert,

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:32.810
especially if you think
the enemy's in the area.

00:11:34.465 --> 00:11:38.190
[machine gun blasting]

00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:39.670
If I had a Minigun in the jungle,

00:11:39.670 --> 00:11:41.010
I would be doing that with it.

00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:42.700
So that's about as accurate as this scene.

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:43.970
Someone might have an M16,

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:45.620
and they're just blasting off rounds

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:48.349
and it's just coming out as
fast as the trigger can go,

00:11:48.349 --> 00:11:50.270
and they would never do a magazine change,

00:11:50.270 --> 00:11:52.450
and he'll be in this firefight
for, like, 20 minutes.

00:11:52.450 --> 00:11:53.980
Yeah, some of weaponry was correct,

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:55.430
but you would definitely
not be lugging around

00:11:55.430 --> 00:11:57.660
this Gatling gun in the
middle of the jungle.

00:11:57.660 --> 00:11:58.980
Those rounds that he had,

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:00.950
for the amount of
firepower that he put down,

00:12:00.950 --> 00:12:03.020
that would have been,
like, hundreds of pounds.

00:12:03.020 --> 00:12:05.178
So, the goal is to be as light as you can

00:12:05.178 --> 00:12:07.310
for the longest period that you can.

00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:09.540
You need to be very
aerodynamic and nimble.

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:11.320
I would rate this a two out of 10,

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:13.094
but a 10 out of 10 for awesome.

00:12:15.119 --> 00:12:17.960
[guns blasting]

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:19.143
This movie is based on

00:12:19.143 --> 00:12:20.600
one of the battalions here in the 25th.

00:12:20.600 --> 00:12:22.270
It's based off 1/27 Infantry,

00:12:22.270 --> 00:12:24.450
the Wolfhounds, from
their Charlie Company.

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:27.560
And this was when they had to
be deployed to Guadalcanal.

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:29.560
This is the type of terrain you'd see

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:30.640
when you're coming out of a jungle

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:32.580
and getting into kind of
the mountainous terrain,

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:34.570
where it's just, like, a
bunch of overgrown grass.

00:12:34.570 --> 00:12:37.055
But they're trying to do a
frontal assault up a hill,

00:12:37.055 --> 00:12:39.217
which was dumb in the first place.

00:12:39.217 --> 00:12:40.283
[grenade pin pings]

00:12:41.159 --> 00:12:42.350
[grenade exploding]

00:12:43.330 --> 00:12:44.630
Probably wouldn't have happened.

00:12:44.630 --> 00:12:48.150
Not really sure why he had
a grenade taped to his belt.

00:12:48.150 --> 00:12:49.741
Yeah, normally you won't
be able to just pull

00:12:49.741 --> 00:12:51.191
the pin itself

00:12:51.191 --> 00:12:53.000
and the grenade still be on your body.

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:54.224
The distance was right,

00:12:54.224 --> 00:12:55.640
the scenario was right,

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:57.680
just the way that it was executed

00:12:57.680 --> 00:12:59.741
probably wouldn't happen in real life.

00:12:59.741 --> 00:13:02.326
Keck: I blew my butt off!

00:13:02.326 --> 00:13:03.840
I blew my butt off!

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:05.770
Steven: This is a pretty
realistic war movie.

00:13:05.770 --> 00:13:07.517
I mean, even all the
way down to the way that

00:13:07.517 --> 00:13:09.210
soldiers act whenever someone gets hurt.

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:12.511
It's still just a bunch of
guys trying to figure it out.

00:13:12.511 --> 00:13:16.855
I would give the scene a
seven out of 10 for realism.

00:13:16.855 --> 00:13:18.885
Soldier: I want you to
get me all the air support

00:13:18.885 --> 00:13:20.625
you can get me on that mountain.

00:13:20.625 --> 00:13:23.673
[helicopters whirring]

00:13:23.673 --> 00:13:25.709
Steven: That was pretty
convenient that they had

00:13:25.709 --> 00:13:28.030
two helicopters and an airplane on station

00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:29.880
within, like, a second of the phone call

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:31.810
going out, or the radio transmission.

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:33.750
And they didn't even
have the grid out yet.

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:34.720
They were, like, still talking

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:36.100
and they were starting to shoot.

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:37.730
So, the reason that
they're using helicopters

00:13:37.730 --> 00:13:40.220
and we decided to use
helicopters during Vietnam

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:42.448
is 'cause one of the only methods

00:13:42.448 --> 00:13:45.160
to truly infill and exfill
from that type of environment

00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:47.460
is to find that one little clearing

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:50.440
and then try to get as many
troops rallied to the same point

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:52.615
so that you can push from there.

00:13:52.615 --> 00:13:55.261
The Vietnamese clearly
knew that that was gonna be

00:13:55.261 --> 00:13:57.149
a point that they would try to infill,

00:13:57.149 --> 00:14:01.109
and then they were dug into
the side of the jungle mountain

00:14:01.109 --> 00:14:03.218
and then just decided to
fight down from there.

00:14:03.218 --> 00:14:07.910
[guns blasting]
[soldiers yelling]

00:14:07.910 --> 00:14:09.160
Everything is pretty accurate.

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:11.059
As soon as they hit the ground,

00:14:11.059 --> 00:14:14.009
they just started running
to the nearest cover,

00:14:14.009 --> 00:14:15.660
which was pretty far away,

00:14:15.660 --> 00:14:17.460
'cause they got dropped in
the middle of a large field.

00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:19.842
This is based off a real event,

00:14:19.842 --> 00:14:22.031
Col. Harold Moore and the 1st Cav.

00:14:22.031 --> 00:14:25.701
[helicopter whirring]

00:14:26.943 --> 00:14:28.477
So, when a helicopter is coming down,

00:14:28.477 --> 00:14:30.841
their entire body is spinning

00:14:30.841 --> 00:14:33.820
and then they're kind of
oscillating all around.

00:14:33.820 --> 00:14:36.468
So you don't even know
really where it's gonna land,

00:14:36.468 --> 00:14:37.390
so you just don't even,

00:14:37.390 --> 00:14:39.457
you hope that you would just
run in the right direction.

00:14:39.457 --> 00:14:41.474
But as far as being in that helicopter,

00:14:41.474 --> 00:14:43.970
that's just kind of hold
on and hope for the best.

00:14:43.970 --> 00:14:45.840
I would give this a 10 out of 10

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:49.310
because I can visualize
what it would have been like

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:50.380
to be in this scenario,

00:14:50.380 --> 00:14:52.710
and I just feel bad for everyone

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:54.110
that had to go through this.

00:14:55.901 --> 00:14:57.470
[soldier yelling]

00:14:57.470 --> 00:14:58.770
So, I love this scene a lot

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:00.760
because they're about to shoot a mortar,

00:15:00.760 --> 00:15:04.550
and they're about to do
it without a sight on it.

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:06.890
And that's not the correct
way that you do it.

00:15:06.890 --> 00:15:08.307
And then whenever they actually

00:15:08.307 --> 00:15:09.823
hang the round and then shoot it,

00:15:09.823 --> 00:15:11.540
it immediately blasts.

00:15:11.540 --> 00:15:12.450
You see the blast.

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:14.558
So the time of flight
would have been, like,

00:15:14.558 --> 00:15:16.230
the same as just a regular bullet.

00:15:16.230 --> 00:15:18.075
The way a mortar works,
so, it has to come out,

00:15:18.075 --> 00:15:20.376
go in the air, and then come down and hit.

00:15:20.376 --> 00:15:21.860
That's not happening in this scene.

00:15:21.860 --> 00:15:23.770
Also, I think that
they're giving corrections

00:15:23.770 --> 00:15:25.150
without a sight on there.

00:15:25.150 --> 00:15:27.719
So I'm not sure what
they're exactly correcting,

00:15:27.719 --> 00:15:29.290
but that wouldn't happen.

00:15:29.290 --> 00:15:31.374
There's a little tank running
around the battlefield.

00:15:31.374 --> 00:15:32.820
Somebody would have probably already tried

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:34.136
to take that guy out.

00:15:34.136 --> 00:15:35.502
[explosion blasting]

00:15:39.012 --> 00:15:40.820
That's pretty accurate to what it is like

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:43.480
to see a tank get shot by another tank.

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:45.660
We had Shermans onshore at Guadalcanal,

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:48.810
and they were just trying
to reinforce the infantry.

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:51.591
But that's kind of the role
of a tank in this situation,

00:15:51.591 --> 00:15:54.760
is just to try to flush
out all the larger targets,

00:15:54.760 --> 00:15:58.030
so the ground forces can
continue to push forward.

00:15:58.030 --> 00:16:00.170
But actually, at this stage of the war,

00:16:00.170 --> 00:16:01.830
tanks weren't on ground yet.

00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:04.546
'Cause we had just taken the beach.

00:16:04.546 --> 00:16:06.696
[guns blasting]

00:16:10.840 --> 00:16:12.520
That's also super accurate.

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:14.763
All the weapons are pretty spot-on.

00:16:14.763 --> 00:16:16.410
The equipment was spot-on.

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:17.910
They're shooting Thompsons,

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:19.910
which is accurate for officers.

00:16:19.910 --> 00:16:22.410
And the officer in this
scene has a Thompson.

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:24.352
This is a good scene,
other than the mortar.

00:16:24.352 --> 00:16:27.823
I would also rate this a seven out of 10.

00:16:27.823 --> 00:16:30.800
Soldier: Got the cheap
designer sunglasses.

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:32.290
Soldier: You sure that's him?

00:16:32.290 --> 00:16:34.500
Steven: This looks like
a Kodak camera zoom.

00:16:34.500 --> 00:16:35.864
I'm not sure what that is.

00:16:37.347 --> 00:16:38.608
Soldier: He's moving.

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:41.960
Steven: You're always gonna have a spotter

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:43.043
and then you're gonna have a sniper,

00:16:43.043 --> 00:16:44.866
and then they're gonna
be talking back and forth

00:16:44.866 --> 00:16:47.393
about, you know, distance
and wind correction.

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:55.403
So, he just said,

00:16:55.403 --> 00:16:58.119
"Two people, go walk down a hill."

00:16:58.119 --> 00:17:01.460
Uh, [laughs] I'm not really
sure why he did that,

00:17:01.460 --> 00:17:03.270
especially since five seconds ago

00:17:03.270 --> 00:17:05.190
they were talking to each
other in normal voices,

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:07.040
giving each other corrections.

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:08.220
And then now they decide

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:10.100
that they want to be
stealthy and walk away.

00:17:10.100 --> 00:17:11.570
You never want to give up on stealth

00:17:11.570 --> 00:17:12.848
just because of distance.

00:17:12.848 --> 00:17:15.681
[leaves rustling]

00:17:18.930 --> 00:17:20.810
First things I tell
people about the jungle,

00:17:20.810 --> 00:17:22.090
especially at nighttime,

00:17:22.090 --> 00:17:24.930
it's almost so loud that it's unbearable.

00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:27.578
So, the first night I
spent inside of a jungle,

00:17:27.578 --> 00:17:29.770
this bird hadn't made this sound all day

00:17:29.770 --> 00:17:31.000
and I didn't know what it was,

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:32.272
but at nighttime,

00:17:32.272 --> 00:17:33.655
I still didn't even know
what type of bird it was.

00:17:33.655 --> 00:17:35.530
It just sounded like this man

00:17:35.530 --> 00:17:37.550
that was just, like, being
stabbed or something.

00:17:37.550 --> 00:17:39.823
It was just like this, "Ah, ah!"

00:17:39.823 --> 00:17:42.180
And I couldn't, I was
like, "What is happening?"

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:44.530
And then all of the other
animals start feeding off that.

00:17:44.530 --> 00:17:45.655
And then it's just,

00:17:45.655 --> 00:17:47.722
it's deafening, how loud the jungle is.

00:17:47.722 --> 00:17:50.220
I would give this a seven out of 10.

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:52.820
The biggest thing that movies
get wrong about the jungle

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:55.710
is how long it actually takes to maneuver

00:17:55.710 --> 00:17:57.783
from one location to the next.

00:17:57.783 --> 00:17:59.460
You might watch a movie and it's like,

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:02.330
they're just walking for
days across the jungle.

00:18:02.330 --> 00:18:03.640
Depending on the type of environment,

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:05.912
you might only move a
football field's length,

00:18:05.912 --> 00:18:06.745
100 meters.

